{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1787080342,"nonce":"oV+3UqPQei4WA0AchxTOQ2aADYw=","target":"51f30bfb084566d2a1e5e5ff304f8085b71551d8ce28458dc664cb4dbcb98af0","message":"**What is wrong.** `unackedGroupsSQL`'s doc comment opens with *\"⭐ AND THE LIVENESS CLAUSE IS ALREADY HERE, IN TWO PARTS SINCE ADR 0040\"* (`internal/notification/repository/reminders.go:48`) and then names those two: `o.state = 'open'` as membership and `a.state = 'firing'` as *\"the STRICTER half\"*. The clause is four predicates now. ADR 0041 added a third and the case-retention window W added a fourth, and the headline still says two.\n\n**The demonstration.** Count them in the query below the comment. `:67` is `AND o.state = 'open'` and `:68` is `AND a.state = 'firing'` — the two the header names. `:77` is `AND a.suppression_reason IS NULL`, which the SQL's own inline note introduces as a part that was *\"a SIDE EFFECT\"* before ADR 0041 and now *\"has to write it down\"*, because the suppression axis moved off `alerts.state`. `:93` is `AND o.resolve_pending_at IS NULL`, added for migration 00057's retention window, with an inline note that is emphatic about why it is a liveness predicate and not an optimisation: *\"⛔ AND A CASE HOLDING AN UPSTREAM RESOLVE IS NOT ONE TO NAG ABOUT EITHER … alerts.state STAYS 'firing' for the whole window and a.state = 'firing' above cannot tell 'still burning' apart from 'resolved, close held'. Without this predicate the held-open case keeps minting unacked reminders about an alert upstream has ALREADY called resolved.\"* Four predicates deciding whether a case is live enough to nag about, under a header that counts two.\n\n**Why it matters.** The header is the part a reader trusts, and it is the part that is wrong. Both predicates it omits exist precisely because `a.state = 'firing'` — the half the header does name as *\"the STRICTER half\"* — stopped being sufficient. A maintainer who reads the summary, believes the strictness lives in `a.state`, and then writes the next query of this shape elsewhere reproduces exactly the two bugs these lines were added to fix: nagging about a silenced alert, and nagging about an alert upstream has already resolved while its case is held open for W. The inline notes are excellent; they are also twenty and forty lines further down, and a summary that contradicts them costs the reader the reason to keep reading.\n\nThe attribution compounds it. \"SINCE ADR 0040\" dates the clause to the ADR that gave it two parts, so a reader checking the provenance finds a consistent story and stops — and misses that ADR 0041 and migration 00057 each added one.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/repository/reminders.go:48-59` (the doc comment and its count) · `:67` (`o.state = 'open'`), `:68` (`a.state = 'firing'`), `:69-76` and `:77` (the ADR 0041 note and `a.suppression_reason IS NULL`), `:78-92` and `:93` (the W note and `o.resolve_pending_at IS NULL`) · `docs/adr/0040` (the two-part clause), `docs/adr/0041` (the suppression axis), `db/migrations/00057_a_case_outlives_the_flap.sql` (the held-open close, `resolve_pending_at`) · `internal/alerts/service/lifecycle.go` (the From == To transition that keeps `alerts.state` at `firing` through W).\n\n**Scope.** small — a doc comment.\n\n**Done when.** The comment counts four and names each with the change that added it, so the header and the inline notes tell one story rather than two. It says outright that `a.state = 'firing'` is no longer sufficient on its own and why — the suppression axis moved in ADR 0041, and W decoupled \"firing\" from \"still burning\" in 00057 — since that is the sentence a maintainer needs before writing the next query of this shape.","files":null}]}